<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-14" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Extra Work and Energy Problems</title>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A uniform rope of mass density <m>\lambda</m> per unit length is coiled on a smooth horizontal table. 
        One end is pulled straight up with constant speed <m>v_0</m> , as shown
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="75% 25%">
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Calculate the the mechanical energy <m>E=K+U</m> of the rope+Earth
                    system.
                    Express your final answer as a function of time using <m>~{y=v_0t}</m>
                    (remember that <m>U</m> uses <m>y_{CM}</m>, not <m>y</m> ).
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Calculate <m>\dfrac{dE}{dt}</m> 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The force <m>F</m> that the hand needs to exert to keep this going
                    can be shown to be <m>~{F=\lambda gy+\lambda v^2}</m>. Compare
                    <m>~{P=Fv_0}</m> to <m>\dfrac{dE}{dt}</m>. Explain your result.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <image source="images\activities\lec10\rope.png">
            <shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        A spool of radius <m>R</m> is pulled by means of a force of 
        magnitude <m>T</m> applied to a string wrapped around an axle of 
        radius <m>r=R/2</m>. The spool is in contact with a horizontal table 
        that applies a friction force of magnitude <m>f</m>. 
        The spool rolls without slipping. When we talk about rotations, 
        you will be able to solve this problem completely and show that 
        <m>f=0</m> when the force is applied at this special position. 
        Right now, take this for granted.
    </p>
    <image width="50%" source="images\activities\lec14\spool.png">
        <shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                If the wheel rolls without slipping, argue that the speed of the
                contact point where <m>T</m> acts is
                <m>~{V_{hand}=\frac{3}{2}V_{CM}}</m>
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Once the spool has moved forward a distance <m>R</m>, what is
                <m>V_{CM}</m>?
                (Hint: Impulse-Momentum + Constant Acceleration)
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Use the Work-Energy Principle to find the kinetic energy
                of the spool once it has moved forward a distance <m>R</m>.
                Why is this not just <m>~{K=\frac{1}{2}MV_{CM}^2}</m>?
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
    <p>
    Consider the U-tube shown below, which is filled
    with an incompressible liquid of density <m>\rho</m>. When the water 
    level is the same on both sides, the fluid is in equilibrium. Suppose that 
    the fluid is instead pushed up to one side like in the figure. Since the
    liquid is incompressible, the whole <q>fluid tube</q> moves with the same 
    speed <m>v</m>. We will ignore any friction and viscosity, so conservation 
    of energy can guide our approach.
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <title>Discussion Questions</title>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do you expect the motion of the liquid to look like?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The zero of potential energy is arbitrary. In terms of the
                    coordinate system shown below, is there a convenient choice for
                    where we should set <m>U=0</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The equilibrium point at <m>~{z=0}</m> is stable. If you draw the potential
                    energy curve <m>~{U(z)}</m>, qualitatively, what does it look like?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </task>
    <task>
        <title>Calculations</title>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write down the kinetic energy as a function of the area 
                    <m>A</m> of the tube, the length <m>\ell</m>, the fluid 
                    density <m>\rho</m>, and the derivative
                    <m>~{\dot{z}=\dfrac{dz}{dt}}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Setting <m>U(z=0)=0</m>, calculate <m>U(z)</m>.
                    Remember that <m>U</m> uses the height of the CM.
                    Hint: think about how much water moved where,
                    that will give you an idea of how the potential
                    energy changed.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the frequency of small oscillations for the water.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>    
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>